  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical evidence is drawn from a historical case study of a single small town in former East Germany, which may limit direct generalizability to other post-socialist urban contexts.",
      "what": "This study traces the spatial and functional evolution of green spaces in the social housing estates of Leinefelde, Germany, across two distinct political eras separated by the Wende. Using an interpretive historical analysis of GDR-era primary sources, including the journal Deutsche Architektur, the research documents how a socialist collective landscape was restructured into a fragmented system of green spaces through 1990s master planning and 2000s housing-scale interventions in the Plattenbau fabric.",
      "why": "It demonstrates how urban green space is not a politically neutral element but a dynamic medium that materializes shifting ideological discourses, showing how state-led welfare policies and post-reunification restructuring physically redefined the boundaries between public and private dwelling spaces."
    }
